      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="carpet-problems" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Carpet Changes</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill on a hard floor needs a mat mainly to protect the floor and catch sweat. On carpet,
            the floor is soft and the risks are different. Our guide to putting a
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill on carpet</a>
            covers the whole installation; these are the four problems a mat is meant to address.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rocking and uneven feet.</strong> A treadmill sits on four or more small feet, and each
            presses its own dent into the pile. If the dents are not equal, the frame twists slightly, and a
            twisted frame is one of the commoner reasons a belt drifts to one side. A firm mat spreads each
            foot's load over a larger area of carpet, so the dents are shallower and more even.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Fibres in the motor.</strong> A running belt and a motor fan both move air, and on carpet
            that air carries loose fibres. Over months they collect in the motor compartment, where they hold
            heat and dust. A mat that covers the whole footprint keeps the carpet surface away from the area
            directly under the motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Airflow under the machine.</strong> Many treadmills draw cooling air through openings in
            the motor hood or the base. Deep pile pressed up close to the underside of the machine restricts
            that air and feeds it fibres. Check your manual for where the vents are, and keep them clear of
            pile; a flat mat helps, and so does a vacuum of the
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">motor compartment</a>
            a few times a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Carpet damage.</strong> A treadmill can weigh 200 to 300 lb, and the dents its feet leave
            in pile and underlay can be permanent. A mat will not stop the carpet compressing, but it spreads
            the load and protects the fibres from sweat, belt lubricant and the scuffing of a frame that moves
            slightly with every stride.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rigid-vs-soft" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Rigid or Soft: Why Firm Mats Win on Carpet</h2>
<p>
            Most mats sold as treadmill mats are high-density PVC foam or EVA foam. They are designed for hard
            floors, where a little softness absorbs vibration and stops the machine scratching wood. On
            carpet, the softness is already there. Adding a second soft layer does not cancel the first; it
            adds to it, and the frame can sink and rock more, not less.
          </p>
          <p>
            What carpet needs is the opposite: a mat that is dense and heavy enough to stay flat and spread the
            load. In practice that means one of two materials.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recycled rubber.</strong> Heavy for its thickness, slow to compress and grippy on both
            faces, so it does not creep across the carpet. The trade-offs are weight, which makes it awkward to
            move, and the smell recycled rubber usually has when it is new. The Rubber-Cal mat is our pick of
            this type.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Solid vinyl.</strong> Thinner and lighter than rubber, but because it is a solid sheet
            rather than a foam, it does not compress the way PVC foam does. The SuperMats 29GS and 30GS are
            this type. A solid vinyl mat is a good choice on low-pile carpet and in rooms where a heavy rubber
            mat would be hard to handle.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Foam has a place</strong> under a light walking pad on low-pile carpet, where the load is
            small and the main aim is to keep sweat and dust off the carpet. That is why the GymCope mat is
            on this list. It is not what we would put under a 250 lb running treadmill on thick carpet.
          </p>
          <p>
            Thickness matters less than density here. A thin dense mat often performs better on carpet than a
            thick soft one; our guide to
            <a href="/how-thick-should-a-treadmill-mat-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how thick a treadmill mat should be</a>
            covers the figures for every floor type.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pile-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Matching the Mat to Your Carpet Pile</h2>
<p>
            The pile — how long and dense the carpet fibres are — decides whether a mat is enough on its own.
            Press a coin into the carpet with your thumb: if it sinks out of sight, treat the carpet as deep.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Carpet</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical example</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What to use</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Low pile, firm underlay</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Office carpet, berber, carpet tiles</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Any dense mat; solid vinyl or rubber</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Medium pile</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Most bedroom and living-room carpet</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Rubber mat; re-level after two weeks</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Deep pile or shag</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Plush or saxony carpet</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A rigid board under a rubber mat</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Any pile, thick foam underlay</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Memory-foam or extra-thick underlay</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A rigid board under a rubber mat</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>When you need a board.</strong> On deep pile or soft underlay, a flexible mat simply
            follows the carpet down. The standard fix is a sheet of plywood or MDF, around 18 mm (about 3/4
            in.) thick, cut a few inches larger than the machine's footprint on every side, with the rubber
            mat laid on top. That gives the feet a single flat plane. Ease or tape the board's edges so they
            are not a trip hazard, and make it large enough that it does not rock on its own.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you use, carpet keeps compressing under a new load for some time. Level the machine with
            its adjustable feet on day one, and check again after about two weeks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="static-and-airflow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Static, Lint and Airflow</h2>
<p>
            Two complaints come up again and again from people running on carpet: static shocks, and dust
            and fibres collecting around the machine. A mat helps with one more than the other.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Static.</strong> Static builds up as the belt slides over the deck and your shoes strike
            the belt, and it is worse in dry air. Carpet makes it worse because many synthetic carpets
            charge readily. Rubber and vinyl mats are insulators, so a mat on its own does not drain charge
            away and is not a static cure. What helps is raising the room's humidity, plugging the machine
            into a properly grounded outlet, and following the manufacturer's advice on grounding. Our guide
            to <a href="/treadmill-static-shock/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill static shock</a>
            covers the fixes in order. Do not use an anti-static spray on the belt itself unless the treadmill's
            maker says it is safe, because some sprays affect belt grip.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lint.</strong> Here a mat does help, by covering the carpet under and around the machine so
            fewer loose fibres are lifted into the air. Choose a mat that extends at least a few inches beyond
            the frame on every side, vacuum around it weekly, and lift the motor hood to vacuum inside once or
            twice a year, with the machine unplugged.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Airflow.</strong> If your treadmill has vents underneath, a mat that lies flat keeps pile
            from pressing against them. Do not stack several soft mats to gain height: the stack compresses
            unevenly and makes rocking worse. If a vent is blocked by the mat itself, the mat is the wrong size
            or the machine's feet need raising, which is a question for the maker.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Sizing a Mat for a Carpeted Room</h2>
<p>
            Measure the machine's full footprint, including the rear rollers and any feet that sit outside
            the frame, then add a margin. On carpet, a bigger margin is better than on hardwood, for two
            reasons: it keeps fibres further from the motor, and a mat whose edge falls just under a foot will
            tilt that foot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Full-size folding treadmills</strong> are typically 70 to 80 in. long and 33 to 37 in. wide.
            A 48 x 90 in. mat such as the Rubber-Cal covers most of them with room to spare.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Long or commercial-style machines</strong> can exceed 80 in. The SuperMats 29GS, at 102 in.,
            is one of the few mats long enough. Check the width: at 36 in. it is narrower than some large
            machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Compact treadmills and walking pads</strong> suit 72 in. or shorter mats. A 30 x 72 in. mat
            is too short for most full-size running treadmills, so do not buy one on price alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Leave clear space behind the treadmill as well. Most makers specify a run-off zone behind the
            belt, commonly about 2 m (roughly 6.5 ft), and the mat should not create a raised edge in that zone that you could
            trip on if you step off the back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Setting Up a Treadmill on a Carpet Mat</h2>
<p>
            The order matters, because a heavy treadmill is hard to move once it is in place.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>1. Vacuum the carpet thoroughly</strong> where the mat will go, so you are not sealing dust
            and grit under it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>2. Lay a board first if you need one,</strong> then the mat. Unroll a rubber mat a day or
            two before you need it, ideally somewhere ventilated, so it can flatten and lose some of its smell.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>3. Place the treadmill with help,</strong> lifting rather than dragging it across the mat,
            which can ruck the mat up and leave a ridge under one foot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>4. Level the machine</strong> with its adjustable feet, check that the belt runs centred at a
            slow walk, and adjust tracking only as the manual describes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>5. Re-check after two weeks.</strong> The carpet will have settled. Re-level, and re-check the
            belt, before you assume something is wrong with the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Needs Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If your treadmill is on a hard floor,</strong> a softer mat may suit you better; our main
            guide to the <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill mats</a>
            covers hardwood, tile and concrete.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the main problem is noise to a room below,</strong> a carpet mat will help less than you
            hope. Impact noise travels through the structure, and the fixes are heavier isolation pads and
            thicker rubber, not a carpet mat.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you rent,</strong> check whether the carpet is your landlord's. A full-coverage mat on a
            board is the gentlest set-up for the carpet, and worth photographing before and after.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you can move the treadmill to a hard floor,</strong> that is usually the simpler answer. A
            hard, level floor removes the rocking and fibre problems entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need a treadmill mat on carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for most treadmills. On carpet a mat spreads the load of the feet so the frame rocks less, keeps fibres away from the motor, and protects the carpet from sweat and lubricant. It matters more on carpet than on hard floors, where the main reason for a mat is floor protection.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best treadmill mat for carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A dense rubber mat large enough to cover the machine's full footprint, such as the Rubber-Cal 4 x 7.5 ft mat, suits most full-size treadmills on low- or medium-pile carpet. For long machines, the SuperMats 29GS at 102 in. is one of the few mats long enough.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a soft or a hard mat better on carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A firm one. Carpet is already soft, and a soft foam mat adds a second layer for the treadmill to sink into, which can increase rocking. Dense rubber or solid vinyl spreads the load and stays flat. Soft PVC mats are fine under light walking pads on low-pile carpet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a treadmill on thick carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but a mat alone is not enough on deep pile or thick foam underlay. Lay a sheet of plywood or MDF about 3/4 in. thick, a few inches larger than the footprint, with a rubber mat on top. Level the machine, and check it again after about two weeks as the carpet settles.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a treadmill mat stop static on carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on its own. Rubber and vinyl are insulators, so a mat does not drain static charge. Raising the room's humidity, using a properly grounded outlet and following the maker's grounding advice help more. A mat does help with the lint and dust that carpet sends into the motor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a treadmill ruin carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can leave permanent dents in the pile and underlay, especially under a heavy machine. A large, firm mat spreads the load and protects the fibres from sweat and scuffing, and a board spreads it further. Expect some compression wherever a treadmill stands for months.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Treadmill Mats on Carpet</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come from treating carpet like a hard floor.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a soft foam mat for carpet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Soft foam suits hardwood, where it absorbs vibration. On carpet it adds a second soft layer, and the frame can sink and rock more. Choose dense rubber or solid vinyl, and keep soft PVC for light walking pads.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">A mat smaller than the machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">If a foot sits on the mat’s edge, that foot tilts and the frame twists, which can pull the belt off centre. Measure the full footprint including the rear feet, and buy a mat that extends a few inches beyond it on every side.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a mat to level deep pile</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">On plush carpet or memory-foam underlay, a flexible mat follows the carpet down. Put a 3/4 in. plywood or MDF board under the mat, sized larger than the machine, with its edges eased or taped.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not re-levelling after two weeks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Carpet keeps compressing under a new load. A treadmill that was level on day one often is not on day fourteen. Re-level with the adjustable feet and re-check belt tracking before assuming a fault.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

