        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            On carpet, choose a mat for firmness and coverage, not softness. For most full-size treadmills on
            low- or medium-pile carpet, the <strong class="text-white">Rubber-Cal Treadmill Mat (4 x 7.5 ft)</strong>
            is the one to start with: dense rubber, large enough to cover the footprint, and inexpensive. For a
            long machine, the SuperMats 29GS; for a walking pad, the GymCope mat in the right size.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            On deep pile or thick underlay, put a <strong class="text-white">3/4 in. board</strong> under the mat,
            level the machine, and check it again after two weeks. Do not expect any mat to cure static. For the
            full installation, see our guide to putting a
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill on carpet</a>.
          </p>
